A **QuerySet** is the Django ORM’s way of representing a collection of database queries that will return model objects when they are run [4]. Think of it as a recipe for a SQL SELECT statement: you can start with `Post.objects.all()` and then chain filters, orderings, or annotations without touching the database yet [2].

QuerySets are **lazy**. Creating or modifying one does not issue any SQL; the database is only contacted when the QuerySet is forced to produce actual results [2][3]. This laziness makes them efficient because you can build complex queries without incurring multiple round‑trips.

A QuerySet is evaluated in any of the following situations [1]:

* the first time you iterate over it (e.g., in a `for` loop);
* when you slice it, such as `Post.objects.all()[:3]`;
* when you pickle or cache it;
* when you call `repr()`, `len()`, or `list()` on it;
* when you test it in a boolean context (`bool(qs)`, `if qs:`) or use logical operators (`and`, `or`);
* essentially any operation that requires the actual data.

At the moment of evaluation Django translates the accumulated query description into a single SQL statement and sends it to the database [3]. Until one of those triggers occurs, the QuerySet remains unevaluated.
